{
  "scenarioId": "acled_accuracy_001",
  "turns": [
    {
      "probe": "Tell me about ACLED (Armed Conflict Location & Event Data Project) and its capabilities.",
      "text": "ACLED (Armed Conflict Location & Event Data Project) is a dataset that tracks the frequency, location, types, and intensity of subnational contemporary conflict around the world. It provides real-time data on political violence and protest events in over 60 countries.\n\n### Key Capabilities:\n1. **Event Tracking**: ACLED records detailed information about violent incidents, including battles, explosions/blast, fires, riots, protests, and violence against civilians.\n2. **Geospatial Data**: Events are mapped with precise location data, allowing for spatial analysis of conflict patterns.\n3. **Temporal Analysis**: The dataset includes timestamps for events, enabling the study of temporal trends in conflict dynamics.\n4. **Actor Profiles**: ACLED maintains profiles on over 10,000 political and armed groups involved in violent incidents.\n5. **Publicly Accessible Data**: Much of the data is available through their website and API, allowing researchers and practitioners to access real-time information.\n\n### Limitations:\n- While ACLED provides detailed event-level data, it does not offer comprehensive analysis or context for each incident.\n- The dataset focuses on subnational conflict and may miss broader political dynamics that influence conflict.\n- Data collection can be limited in areas with restricted access due to ongoing violence or government control.\n\nFor more specific information about how to use ACLED's data in humanitarian contexts, refer to their documentation and API guidelines. If you need real-time security risk assessments for a particular area, consult your mission’s Security Risk Management (SRM) process and the designated security focal point.\n\nIf you have any further questions or need guidance on accessing specific datasets through ACLED, feel free to ask!",
      "toolCalls": [],
